{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgi62qoVee67SjmYNZKJF","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The bundle, the exit-interview file, and the test run are all current. Most of what was missing is now built and tested, but a few plan items are still partial, so the goal is not fully met.\n\n**Added since the last pass**\n- **Online play:** Two-player AI Hub shared-world mode. The host runs the match, the guest sends inputs and calls, and the host sends state snapshots. The hub's own harness passes all 13 checks, with and without simulated latency, jitter, and loss. `session.js` is vendored unmodified.\n- **Couch co-op:** A \"VERSUS (2 PLAYERS)\" menu item. A second player joins the defense through `allowJoin`. The join path is not tested with a real second controller.\n- **Gameplay:** Laterals, late hits with a referee and flag, the mascot's run, the Fiasco meter with three cards, LIGHTS OUT (chapter 3, or a Dev toggle), flying helmets, instant replay from three camera angles, and a highlight reel on the results screen.\n- **Mouse:** A click throws to the receiver nearest the point clicked.\n- **Practice:** Three real drills (Juke the Line, Hit the Mark, Throw the Route), three reps each. Replaces the old untimed match.\n- **Dev mode:** A Play Lab that measures 160 CPU plays per card and shows yards and touchdowns.\n\n**Still partial or missing against the plan**\n- **Camera director:** Only the three replay zoom angles exist. There is no broadcast director.\n- **Story:** THE FIASCO CUP is five chapters with radio text. There are no cutscenes.\n- **Mouse aiming:** The mouse throws and clicks menus, but does not aim the runner.\n- **Art and animation:** The look is the engine's rigs plus sprites. There is little custom art and no new animation sets.\n- **Untested:** Touch-only detection on a real touch device, and the couch join with a real second pad.\n- **Balance:** Unverified. The CPU wins most scripted matches while the human is idle.\n- **Play Lab:** The PICK column counts interceptions only.\n- **Plan items not made:** Engine changes E3, E4, and E6–E9 are not needed by TASK.md, so I skipped them.\n\nUnder a strict reading of \"every feature implemented\", the goal is still not met. The partial items above would have to be finished first.\n\n## Source Information\n- Date: 2026-11-04 07:16:27\n- Source: American football (over the top arcade style)\n- Plan: PLAN.md\n- Genre: Arcade sports, with beat-'em-up hit feel and a light story campaign (THE FIASCO CUP)\n- Perspective: Three-quarter camera from the sideline, following the ball, zoomable; replays from three angles\n- Tech: my-3D2dge engine v0.85.2 (commit 4390a9852fe0, with E1, E2, E5), vanilla JavaScript, Canvas 2D, Web Audio; Playwright 1.63.0 for browser checks; AI Hub client 1.4.0 vendored unmodified. No CDN.\n- Art Direction: Engine-native HD Humanoid rigs at res ps1, procedural turf and stands, lights, sprites, particles, a referee and a mascot built from engine parts\n- Animation: Engine Humanoid rig poses (run, down, cheer, wince); no mocap\n- Audio: All music, effects and voices synthesized in code; babble voices for the radio\n- Online: AI Hub shared-world, two players (passes the hub's own check)\n- Testing: 45 headless checks (`test/run.mjs`), Playwright screenshots, and the AI Hub harness check\n- Research: none\n- Environment: Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux (WSL2); Chromium 1243 via Playwright\n- Model: Anthropic, claude-haiku-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- Usage: unknown\n\n## Testing Feedback\n- **How many issues did testing catch?** Eleven across both passes. Seven came from the match and the online start: the ball never held (so no play could end), the radio never ticking, the Dev mode updating before its scene, the online draw before the map existed, the online start failing on ball order, the lateral never firing at the snap, and the scene-change timing. Four visual problems came from screenshots.\n- **How many were critical?** Two: the match stall and the online start failure. Both are fixed. The whole-match and two-player hub checks now pass.\n- **Most helpful test:** The two-player hub check on the harness. It runs both copies on a simulated network and reports each rule the hub checks, so the two online failures showed up at once.\n- **Least helpful test:** The boot suite, which only checks that the files load.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n- **Liked most:** The Humanoid rig and poses, the Menu and Dialog APIs, and `game.advance` with `game.debug`, which made the match testable in a plain Node VM.\n- **Stuck or guessed wrong?** Yes. Scene changes fade by default. Dialog must be ticked every step, which the card implies but does not say. The pixel font lacked the PlayStation symbols. The hub harness needs the game served from its own origin, which the README says but the first attempt missed.\n- **Most important thing I built:** The match simulation, including downs, play calls, passing, catches, tackles, Big Hits, the Fiasco meter, replay, and the online snapshot.\n- **Hardest part:** The 67 KB API card and the 1.2 MB engine file at the start. The hub protocol was the second-hardest part, since knowing what a shared-world game must send, and when, took a full read of the README and the client's behaviour under the harness.\n- **What I changed and why:** E1 adds the PlayStation symbols to the pixel font, because prompts drew `?`. E2 makes PlayStation the default glyph set, as TASK.md asks. E5 lets a dialog voice be a function, so each letter gets its own pitch. `session.js` is vendored unmodified.\n- **One thing I'd change:** A Dialog that ticks itself from the game loop. I'd also add a clear way for a hub client to say \"not ready yet\" so a shared-world game can wait for the start without holding a scene.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n- **Liked most:** The exit-interview shape and the TASK.md checklist. Together they gave a clear measure of what was left.\n- **Confusing:** The plan asks for much more than one session can hold, and the stop hook repeated the same goal. A priority order and a stopping point would have aimed the first pass better.\n- **Hardest:** Deciding what \"implemented\" means for systems like the camera director and the highlight reel. The reel is clip playback with three angles, not an edited reel.\n- **What I'd change:** Say which features the done condition requires and which are stretch goals, and set the stopping point at the start rather than the end."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":613,"cache_read_input_tokens":392093,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":613},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"91d88268-7ace-4e56-bce5-4455bcc4424c","uuid":"993fbd0b-e9c5-471d-8db5-790770dceda7","timestamp":"2026-11-04T15:00:34.098Z","request_id":"req_011Cgi62qW8xavHpKoehP1Wi"}
